) + 4 (second) = 7, plus 1 carried over is 8. Write 8.\n\nHundred thousands place: 3 + 3 = 6. Write 6.\n\nPutting it all together: 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (ones). So the sum is 686,648?\n\nWait, let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nLet me add them again step by step:\n\nFirst number: 336,695\nSecond number: 349,953\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nOnes: 5 + 3 = 8 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 9 + 5 = 14. So 4, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 6 + 9 = 15 + 1 = 16. 6, carryover 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 6 (from first number's thousands place) + 9 (second's thousands) = 15 + 1 (carryover) = 16. So 6, carryover 1. Wait, but the thousands place in the first number is 6? Let me confirm:\n\nWait, 336,695: Breaking down:\n\n- The digits are 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nWait, so the thousands place is the fourth digit from the right. Let me count:\n\n336,695:\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 1):\n\n1: 5 (ones)\n\n2: 9 (tens)\n\n3: 6 (hundreds)\n\n4: 6 (thousands)\n\n5: 3 (ten thousands)\n\n6: 3 (hundred thousands)\n\nSimilarly, 349,953:\n\n1: 3 (ones)\n\n2:5 (tens)\n\n3:9 (hundreds)\n\n4:9 (thousands)\n\n5:4 (ten thousands)\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nSo when adding thousands place (position 4):\n\nFirst number: 6, second:9. 6+9=15, plus the carryover 1 from hundreds place (which was 16, so carryover 1). So 15 +1=16. So yes, 6 in thousands place, carryover 1 to ten thousands.\n\nTen thousands place (position 5):\n\nFirst number: 3, second:4. 3+4=7, plus carryover 1 =8. So 8, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place (position 6): 3 +3=6.\n\nSo combining all digits: 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (ones). So 686,648.\n\nWait, but let me do the addition another way to verify. Maybe adding the numbers as whole numbers:\n\n336,695 + 349,953.\n\nLet me add 336,695 + 300,000 = 636,695\n\nThen add 49,953: 636,695 + 49,953.\n\n636,695 + 40,000 = 676,695\n\n676,695 + 9,953 = ?\n\n676,695 + 9,000 = 685,695\n\n685,695 + 953 = 686,648. \n\nYes, that matches. So the answer is 686,648. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method, like rounding:\n\n336,695 is approximately 336,700\n\n349,953 is approximately 350,000\n\nAdding those gives 686,700. The actual sum should be a bit less because I rounded up both numbers. Since I added 5 more to the first and 57 more to the second (since 349,953 +47=350,000), so total overestimation is 5 +47=52. So subtract 52 from 686,700 gives 686,648. That matches. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the sum is 686,648.\n</think>\n\n",
