 can only be a single number, the 12 would actually mean we have a 2 in the hundred thousands and carry over 1 to the millions place. Oh right! I almost forgot that. So actually, the hundred thousands place is 2, and we have an additional 1 in the millions place. \n\nSo putting it all together, the sum should be 1,227,018? Wait, let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let me recount each digit carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 551,067\nSecond number: 675,951\n\nAligning them:\n\n  551067\n+675951\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 7 + 1 = 8. Correct.\n\nTens place: 6 + 5 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1. Okay.\n\nHundreds place: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 (carryover) = 10. Write 0, carryover 1. Right.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 5 = 6 + 1 (carryover) = 7. No carryover. Good.\n\nTen thousands place: 5 + 7 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands place: 5 + 6 = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12. So here, since it's the hundred thousands place, we can't have 12. So we write 2 in the hundred thousands and carryover 1 to the millions place. \n\nSo the millions place is the carried-over 1. So the total is 1 (million) 2 (hundred thousand) 2 (ten thousand) 7 (thousand) 0 (hundred) 1 (ten) 8 (one). So that's 1,227,018. Wait, but let me check with another method to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, adding the numbers as whole numbers:\n\n551,067 + 675,951.\n\nLet me break them down:\n\n551,067 is 500,000 + 50,000 + 1,000 + 60 +7.\n\n675,951 is 600,000 + 70,000 + 5,000 + 900 + 50 +1.\n\nAdding each part:\n\nMillions: 500k + 600k = 1,100,000.\n\nHundred thousands: 50k +70k = 120,000.\n\nTen thousands: 1,000 +5,000 = 6,000.\n\nHundreds: 0 +900 = 900.\n\nTens: 60 +50 = 110.\n\nOnes:7 +1=8.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n1,100,000 + 120,000 = 1,220,000.\n\n1,220,000 +6,000 = 1,226,000.\n\n1,226,000 +900 = 1,226,900.\n\n1,226,900 +110 = 1,227,010.\n\n1,227,010 +8 = 1,227,018. Okay, that matches. So the answer is correct.\n\nWait, but in my first calculation, when I did the ten thousands place, I had 5 (from first number's ten thousands place) and 7 (from second number's ten thousands place). Wait, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, maybe I messed up the place values?\n\nWait, the first number is 551,067. Breaking down:\n\nDigits: 5 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 675,951. Breaking down:\n\n6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSo when adding the ten thousands place: first number's ten thousands digit is 5, second's is 7. So 5+7=12. That's correct. So the ten thousands place would have 2 with a carryover 1 to the hundred thousands. Then the hundred thousands place: 5 +6 =11 plus the carryover 1 makes 12, so 2 with a carryover to millions. So that's correct. \n\nTherefore, the final sum is indeed 1,227,018. Okay, so the reasoning steps are correct. I think that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\n",
