r 1.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 7 = 16, plus 1 is 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 4 + 3 = 7, plus 1 is 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nTen-thousands: 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6.\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 + 9 = 18. Wait, so here, 18. Since it's the hundred-thousands place, adding 9 and 9 gives 18, so we have to carry over. So the hundred-thousands place becomes 8, and carryover 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place. So putting it all together, the sum is 1,868,707?\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts. Let's break them down:\n\n954,931 is 900,000 + 50,000 + 4,000 + 900 + 30 + 1\n\n913,776 is 900,000 + 10,000 + 3,000 + 700 + 70 + 6\n\nAdding each part:\n\nMillions: 900k + 900k = 1,800,000\n\nHundred thousands: 50,000 + 10,000 = 60,000\n\nTen thousands: 4,000 + 3,000 = 7,000? Wait, no, wait. Wait, maybe I need to re-express each number properly.\n\nWait, 954,931 is 9 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, 913,776 is 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSo adding each place:\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 9 = 18 \u2192 8 with carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 5 + 1 = 6, plus the carryover 1 makes 7.\n\nThousands: 4 + 3 = 7.\n\nHundreds: 9 +7 = 16 \u2192 6 with carryover 1.\n\nTens: 3 +7 =10 \u2192 0 with carryover 1.\n\nOnes: 1 +6 =7.\n\nWait, hold on, this contradicts my previous step. Wait, maybe I mixed up the places earlier.\n\nWait, let me do this again carefully:\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:\n\n        9 5 4 9 3 1\n      + 9 1 3 7 7 6\n      ----------------\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes: 1 +6 =7. Write 7.\n\nTens: 3 +7 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 9 +7 =16, plus 1 is 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 4 +3 =7, plus 1 is 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nTen-thousands: 5 +1 =6. Write 6.\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 +9 =18. So here, since it's the hundred-thousands place, adding 9 and 9 gives 18. So we write 8 in the hundred-thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place. Since there's no millions place in the original numbers, the carryover becomes a 1 in the millions place. So the total is 1 (million) 8 (hundred thousand) 6 (ten thousand) 8 (thousand) 7 (hundred) 0 (ten) 7 (one). So that's 1,868,707.\n\nWait, but when I broke it down earlier, I thought the ten thousands place was 5 +1 +1 (carryover) but actually, the carryover from the hundreds place was only 1, so ten thousands place is 5+1=6 plus no carryover from thousands place (since thousands place had 4+3+1=8, no carryover). So that's correct. So the total is indeed 1,868,707.\n\nHmm, but let me check with another method. Let's add 954,931 + 913,776.\n\nAlternatively, 954,931 + 900,000 is 1,854,931. Then add the remaining 13,776: 1,854,931 +13,776 = ?\n\n1,854,931 +10,000 =1,864,931\n\nThen +3,000 =1,867,931\n\nThen +700 =1,868,631\n\n+70 =1,868,701\n\n+6 =1,868,707. Yes, that matches. So the answer is 1,868,707.\n\nWait, but in the initial digit-by-digit breakdown, when I first thought of the hundred thousands place, I thought 9+9=18, so writing 8 and carryover 1, leading to the 1 in the millions place. So that's correct. So the final answer should be 1,868,707. Okay, that seems right. I think that's the correct sum.\n</think>\n\n",
