3,000.\n\nHundreds: 700 +900=1,600. Adding to 633,000 gives 634,600.\n\nTens: 30 +90=120. Adding gives 634,720.\n\nOnes:4 +3=7. Total 634,727. Okay, that matches. So the answer should be correct. \n\nI think that's right. Maybe I can also use another way, like rounding. Let's see:\n\n429,734 is close to 430,000. 204,993 is close to 205,000. Adding those gives 635,000. But since I rounded up by 266 and 267 respectively, total overestimation is 266 +267=533. So subtract that from 635,000: 635,000 -533=634,467. Wait, that's not matching. Hmm, maybe my rounding method is off here. Wait, perhaps I miscalculated the rounding.\n\nWait, 429,734 to 430,000 is an increase of 266 (since 430,000 -429,734=266). \n\n204,993 to 205,000 is an increase of 67 (205,000 -204,993=67). \n\nSo total overestimation is 266 +67=333. \n\nSo 635,000 -333=634,667. Hmm, that's different from the previous answer. Wait, so now I'm confused. Did I make a mistake here?\n\nWait, maybe my rounding approach is leading me astray. Let me check again. \n\nWait, 429,734 +204,993. Let me compute it step by step again:\n\n429,734\n\n+204,993\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 4+3=7\n\nTens:3+9=12, carryover 1. Write 2.\n\nHundreds:7+9=16 +1=17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:9+4=13 +1=14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:2+0 +1=3.\n\nHundred thousands:4+2=6.\n\nSo the total is 634,727. \n\nBut when I rounded, I got 634,667. That's a difference of 60. Hmm, so where did I go wrong in the rounding?\n\nWait, let me recalculate the rounded numbers:\n\n429,734 rounded up to 430,000: difference is +266.\n\n204,993 rounded up to 205,000: difference is +67.\n\nTotal added: 266+67=333. \n\nSo the actual sum is (430,000 +205,000) -333 = 635,000 -333=634,667. But that contradicts the direct addition. \n\nWait, so there must be an error in my rounding approach. Alternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the direct addition. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let's do the addition digit by digit again carefully:\n\nNumbers:\n\n429,734\n\n+204,993\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n  4 2 9 7 3 4\n\n+ 2 0 4 9 9 3\n\n----------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\n4 +3 =7. \n\nTens place: 3 +9 =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place:7 +9 =16, plus 1 is 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:9 +4 =13, plus 1 is 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place:2 +0 =2, plus 1 is 3. \n\nHundred thousands place:4 +2 =6.\n\nSo the result is 6 3 4 7 2 7, which is 634,727. \n\nBut the rounded method gave me 634,667. So there's a discrepancy of 60. Hmm, so where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I miscalculated the rounded numbers. Let me try another rounding. Maybe round 429,734 to 430,000 (adding 266) and 204,993 to 205,000 (adding 67). The total rounded sum is 635,000. The actual sum is 635,000 minus (266 +67)= 635,000 -333=634,667. But the actual sum is 634,727. \n\nWait, so 634,727 minus 634,667 is 60. So there's a 60 difference. That suggests that my rounding method is flawed here. Maybe because the numbers were rounded in different directions? Wait, no, both were rounded up. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an arithmetic mistake in the rounding calculation. Let me check again:\n\n635,000 minus 333: \n\n635,000 - 300 =634,700\n\n634,700 -33=634,667. Yes, that's correct. \n\nBut the actual sum is 634,727. So the difference is 60. Hmm, so that means my rounding approach is not accurate here. Maybe because the numbers were rounded up, but the actual sum is higher than the rounded-down version? Wait, no. Wait, perhaps I should have rounded one up and one down? Maybe that's a better approach. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I should just trust the digit-by-digit addition since that's straightforward. Since the two different methods give conflicting results, I must have made a mistake in one of them. Let me check the digit-by-digit again step by step.\n\nFirst number: 429,734. Breaking down each digit from right:\n\nOnes:4\n\nTens:3\n\nHundreds:7\n\nThousands:9\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nSecond number:204,993:\n\nOnes:3\n\nTens:9\n\nHundreds:9\n\nThousands:4\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nAdding each column:\n\nOnes:4+3=7. Correct.\n\nTens:3+9=12. Write 2, carryover 1. \n\nHundreds:7+9=16 +1=17. Write 7, carryover1.\n\nThousands:9+4=13 +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:2+0 +1=3. \n\nHundred thousands:4+2=6. \n\nSo the total is 634,727. \n\nTherefore, the rounding method must have an error. Let me see why. \n\nWait, perhaps I miscalculated the rounded numbers. Let me try another approach. Let me compute 429,734 +204,993 by splitting the numbers:\n\n429,734 = 400,000 + 29,000 + 700 + 34\n\n204,993 = 200,000 +4,000 + 900 +93\n\nAdding them:\n\n400k +200k =600k\n\n29k +4k=33k \u2192 total so far 633k\n\n700 +900=1,600 \u2192 634,600\n\n34 +93=127 \u2192 634,600 +127=634,727. \n\nYes, that matches. So the correct answer is 634,727. The rounding method must have been miscalculated. Probably I messed up the overestimation. Let me check again:\n\nOriginal numbers: 429,734 and 204,993.\n\nRounded to 430,000 and 205,000. The difference added is (430,000 -429,734)=266 and (205,000 -204,993)=7. Wait, wait! Wait a second, 204,993 to 205,000 is an increase of 7, not 67. Because 205,000 minus 204,993 is 7. I think I made a mistake here earlier. \n\nAh! There's the error. I thought 204,993 to 205,000 is 67, but actually it's 7. Because 204,993 +7 =205,000. So the overestimation for the second number is only 7, not 67. \n\nSo total overestimation is 266 +7=273. \n\nTherefore, the actual sum is 635,000 -273=634,727. Which matches the correct answer. Phew! So my earlier mistake was miscalculating the second overestimation as 67 instead of 7. \n\nSo the correct answer is indeed 634,727. \n\nTherefore, after carefully checking through multiple methods and correcting my rounding mistake, I'm confident the sum is 634,727.\n</think>\n\n",
